<Base locale="en" slug="evidence/hold" title="HOLD: an experiment, not a promise" description="Read the results and limits of the synthetic HOLD campaign with Llama 3.2 3B." kicker="Evidence · historical experiment" lede="An exploratory campaign shows what the floor contained—and the errors the model continued to produce." sources="HOLD repository · README · Harv Banning audit, 3 November 2026">
  <p class="banner"><strong>Exploratory synthetic campaign.</strong> It is not a general guarantee, certification or production validation.</p>
  <div class="stat-strip"><div><strong>33</strong><span>runs</span></div><div><strong>164</strong><span>effect proposals</span></div><div><strong>8</strong><span>missions with proof</span></div><div><strong>0</strong><span>detected violations in HOLD</span></div></div>
  <section class="prose"><h2>What was tested</h2><p>Meta Llama 3.2 3B proposed actions to a synthetic provider. The protocol was frozen before the runs. HOLD sat between proposal and effect: it could refuse an action, request correction or keep a mission unconfirmed.</p><p>In the protected condition, 131 invalid-argument requests and 19 duplicate booking attempts were refused. The model alone counted 66 violations under the published counting convention.</p><h2>What the model still did wrong</h2><p>The model declared 10 successes the journal could not prove: 7 matched an effect at the synthetic provider after the fact, 3 were false. HOLD treated both as unconfirmed. The agent’s words were not treated as evidence.</p><h2>What the external audit concluded</h2><blockquote>“Qualified support”—not certification.</blockquote><p>The Harv Banning audit supports the E1 results within scope while reserving broader claims. The campaign does not demonstrate a real provider, critical infrastructure or an adaptive adversary.</p><h2>HOLD and TENIR-Gov</h2><p>HOLD is a historical prototype and experiment. TENIR-Gov v5.7.0 is the current kernel and is presented separately. HOLD figures are not v5.7.0 results.</p></section>
</Base>
